1. Adam's three cases
   "abc" -> ["abc", "acb", "bac", "bca", "cab", "cba"]
   "aa"  -> ["aa"]
   ""    -> [""], one arrangement of nothing rather than none

2. How many, and the repeats the skip rule removes
   input         n!  distinct  returned
   "abcd"        24        24        24
   "aabb"        24         6         6
   "banana"     720        60        60
   "aaaa"        24         1         1
   distinct = n! divided by the factorial of each letter's count, and the
   skip rule produces exactly that many without generating the rest.

3. Permute characters, not bytes
   "ée" -> ["eé", "ée"]
   the two bytes of "é" swapped: String::from_utf8 is_err = true
   A permutation of bytes can be something that is not text at all.
